<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) && role != null => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i-1))) || (\old(this.get(i-1)) == null && this.get(i) == null));</code>

However, there seems to be a small mistake in the intended logic. The correct interpretation should be that the element at the current position `i` in the new list should be equal to the element at the previous position `i-1` in the old list, starting from the insertion point. Here's the corrected version:

<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) && role != null => \forall int i; index < i && i <= \old(this.size()); this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i-1)));</code>

This ensures that for every valid index `i` from `index + 1` to the old size of the list, the element at position `i` in the new list is equal to the element at position `i-1` in the old list.
